Ambassador Jeffrey Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President. Thank you, Special Envoy Xia, for your briefing. And warm welcome to our colleague from Burundi to the Council this morning.
The United States is deeply concerned by the impacts of the ongoing Ebola outbreak in the DRC.
Safe, unhindered access for health responders and the protection of medical personnel and facilities are critical to saving lives.
We thank all of the humanitarian, security, and peacekeeping personnel risking their lives to help address this crisis.
Colleagues, the Washington Accords for Peace and Prosperity represent a historic opportunity to advance peace, stability, and prosperity in eastern DRC and across the Great Lakes region. Realizing that promise requires the DRC and Rwanda to implement their obligations fully and without delay.
We urge the parties to uphold the ceasefire, halt harmful drone strikes, and refrain from inflammatory rhetoric. All Security Council members should reinforce these expectations and maintain pressure for implementation.
We thank Qatar for its continued facilitation and urge the DRC and AFCM23 – through their newly rebranded alliance the Front for the Refoundation of the Nation – to build on the progress made in Switzerland to conclude the remaining Doha protocols.
We welcome the AU’s mediation under President Gnassingbé of Togo and the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region’s contributions. Close coordination among these efforts and with the Washington and Doha processes remains essential.
The first Expanded Joint Verification Mechanism Plus missions to Minembwe and Masisi demonstrate how impartial verification can help the DRC and AFC/M23 investigate alleged violations and resolve disputes. We commend the UN Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo’s (MONUSCO) essential operational support and urge the parties to facilitate further missions promptly.
The DRC’s transfer of 15 detainees to AFC/M23 under the prisoner exchange protocol is a welcome confidence-building measure. Sustained follow-through on such commitments can build the trust necessary for progress at the negotiating table.
The Joint Security Coordination Mechanism’s October Planning Conference provides an opportunity to define actionable next steps for ensuring both the DRC and Rwanda implement their Washington Accords obligations. We urge the two parties to use this mechanism to exchange information and advance agreed operations.
Within the DRC, progress towards the Democratic Forces for the Liberation of Rwanda (FDLR) neutralization must continue. Any current cooperation between the FDLR and elements of the Congolese armed forces must cease immediately.
The repatriation of more than 120 FDLR ex-combatants and family members, supported by MONUSCO, represents tangible progress. The DRC government’s recent plan for further demobilization has the potential to be an encouraging step.
We urge the DRC to clarify the operational details of its proposed plan and begin implementation promptly. Coordination with MONUSCO and regional and international partners will be critical to translating that plan into results.
Progress on FDLR neutralization must be matched by Rwanda’s fulfillment of its own obligations. Rwanda must immediately and unconditionally withdraw its troops and military equipment from the DRC and cease all support to M23.
The United States will continue to take action against those who obstruct peace, abuse civilians, or profit from the illegal exploitation of the DRC’s natural resources.
The United States remains committed to supporting peace, security, and prosperity across the Great Lakes. We look to all parties to consolidate recent gains through sustained cooperation and verifiable action.
I thank you.
