Ambassador Jeffrey Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President. And thank you to Special Representative Jenča for your briefing. We warmly welcome Foreign Minister Bula and congratulate President De la Espriella and his administration on their election.
I have to say: across every level of this new government, we see exceptional leadership, renewed determination, and a commitment to putting the Colombian people first. They are only in the early innings of their serious work, but it is clear that the Colombian people are remembering what their great nation is capable of after four dark years under Gustavo Petro.
But I want to recognize something else President De la Espriella did—or rather something he didn’t do. He didn’t come to New York for UNGA. He promised the Colombian people that he would remain in his country rather than spend his presidency traveling the international diplomatic circuit. And so far, he’s kept that promise. While his capable Vice President represented Colombia here, President De la Espriella stayed home, attending to the needs of his people.
Colleagues, there is no shortage of speeches about leadership in this building. Colombia’s new president demonstrated leadership without ever setting foot here. He understands something we should all remember: our first responsibility is not to the diplomatic circuit, it is to the people we serve. I wish more leaders had that kind of resolve, the same resolve that propelled President Trump to the White House, and the same resolve that has kept him and us delivering for the American people.
Now, Colombia’s new administration has already been tested. Just three days after their President took office, a devastating 7.4-magnitude earthquake struck Colombia, claiming more than 330 lives and affecting nearly half a million people. The UN itself estimates more than $13 billion in physical destruction. In response to the earthquake, the United States has provided over $26.5 million in disaster assistance, and we urge our partners to provide support as well. As Colombia focuses on recovery efforts, we stand ready to continue investment in Colombia’s infrastructure to help rebuild.
Colleagues, Colombia’s full implementation of its commitments under the 2016 Peace Accord remains essential to the country’s future, but lasting peace requires something even more fundamental: the Colombian government—not the cartels, not the narcotraffickers, not the terrorists—must control Colombian territory. The previous administration promised “Total Peace.” Instead, armed groups expanded their territory, grew their ranks, and diversified their criminal enterprises. Instead of “Total Peace” total impunity reigned in much of Colombia. Some ambiguous “peace” cannot be an excuse to give terrorists room to recruit, rearm, and terrorize innocent families.
President De la Espriella understands that. We welcome his decision to end the failed negotiations inherited from the Petro administration and confront the criminal organizations that have preyed on Colombians and Americans alike. The United States looks forward to deepening our security partnership with his government. Criminals have no safe harbor in the United States—and they should have no safe harbor in Colombia.
Finally, as the Council approaches the October 31 expiration of the UN Verification Mission’s mandate, we welcome Colombia’s determination to take ownership of rural reform while preserving the mission’s essential verification responsibilities and field presence. This should always be our goal here on the Council. We should support national leadership, focus on core mandates, and deliver results rather than perpetuate missions indefinitely.
Colleagues, a stronger Colombia means a safer hemisphere. Under President De la Espriella, Colombia is once again showing the world what determined, patriotic leadership looks like. The United States is proud to stand with the Colombian people and their new government. We look forward to what we can accomplish together.
I thank you.
