Ambassador Jeffrey Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President. Thank you to Special Envoy Grundberg, Mr. Yahya, and Ms. Ashing for your briefings.
The United States is gravely concerned by the continued escalation by the terrorist Houthis. We strongly condemn Houthi attacks against civilian infrastructure and airports, which have resulted in fatalities and injuries, including most recently the Houthi missile strikes on King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh. These outrageous attacks reflect a broader pattern of deplorable Houthi behavior and blatant disregard for civilian life.
The Houthis have intensified attacks on critical energy infrastructure, including claimed strikes on Saudi Aramco facilities that threaten global energy security and economic stability far beyond the region.
The pattern remains unmistakable: the Houthis act as instruments of Tehran to threaten freedom of navigation, undermine regional stability, and terrorize innocent civilians. We demand that the Houthis cease their attacks against the legitimate governments of Yemen and Saudi Arabia, and we demand that Iran cease all support to the Houthis.
In the face of the Houthi threat, the United States stands firmly with the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia and the Government of Yemen as they defend their sovereignty and work to restore stability.
The Houthis also rely on a flow of dual-use components and precursor chemicals. When this Council renews the Yemen sanctions regime in mid-November, it must act on the Panel of Expert’s long-standing recommendations to take steps to cut off the flow of these items to the Houthis.
Turning to the humanitarian situation, we are concerned by the Houthis’ role in the mounting civilian atrocities. Months before their recent aggressive activity, the Houthis closed humanitarian space through harassment of aid agencies and detention of humanitarian workers.
October 19 will mark the fifth anniversary of the beginning of the Houthi detention of U.S. embassy staff in Sana’a, an act of calculated cruelty. We reiterate our demand for the immediate and unconditional release of unjustly detained UN and U.S. embassy staff.
Going forward, the United States calls on the Security Council to press for full implementation of Resolutions 2140 and 2216 and to hold Iran accountable.
The United States will continue working with our partners to counter the Houthi threat, defend our navigational rights and freedoms, and support long-term regional stability.
I thank you.
