Ambassador Jeffrey Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you to the briefers. The United States appreciates your leadership and cooperation.
Coordination and partnership between the UN and AU can help achieve peace, security, and prosperity in Africa.
With UN, AU, and regional peace operations active in some of the most fragile parts of Africa, we have a shared interest in ensuring our doctrine, policies, and directives afford effective cooperation.
We agree with the secretary-general on the collective need to focus on ensuring the partnership is mutually reinforcing, more responsive, and more effective for the people they serve.
The crisis in Sudan, insecurity in the Sahel, and the situation in eastern DRC underline this point.
On Sudan, we continue to call for an immediate end to the senseless violence and to support a humanitarian truce. At the same time, we welcome and support the efforts of the UN and AU, as integral members of the Quintet, to coordinate an inclusive political dialogue for the Sudanese people.
We continue to consider all ways to support these efforts, including through an expansion of the Darfur arms embargo to cover all of Sudan.
Turning to the DRC, both the AU and UN should continue to facilitate the U.S. and Qatar-led peace processes to resolve the persistent conflict and bring prosperity to all.
On the Sahel, the United States condemns in the strongest terms terrorist attacks, including by drones, conducted in Burkina Faso, Mali, and Niger.
AU-UN cooperation and African leadership are critical to addressing a multitude of challenges faced by the people of West Africa and the Sahel. We, as the international community, have a vested interest in countering terrorism and the threat it poses to the global community.
On peace and security in Africa more broadly, UNSCR 2719, which provides a framework for UN financing of Security Council-authorized, AU-led peace support operations, can be a potentially useful tool.
The deployment of a peace operation under UNSCR 2719 will strengthen the AU-UN partnership and leverage the AU’s comparative advantages to addressing conflict in Africa.
Any such peace operation should be small, time bound, and have clear objectives. We will continue to evaluate, on a case-by-case basis, where 2719 might be most effectively applied.
