Ambassador Elisabeth Millard
U.S. Senior Adviser for European and Eurasian Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam Chair.
During the previous session, The Gambia and this Committee’s Bureau led the Second Committee through a long-overdue reckoning.
Through the passage of the decision on Revitalization of the work of the 2nd Committee, this Committee has proven that institutional reform is possible. We strongly urge all other committees—from First to Sixth—to look to the Second Committee and follow this lead in eliminating waste.
But now comes the real test. We must ensure these efforts were not in vain. We are at the starting line, not the finish line.
The United States strongly supports continued common-sense reforms that prioritize efficiency, accountability, and good stewardship of resources.
For too long, the UN has drifted from its core mission, bogged down by a culture that prioritizes process over performance… paperwork over real-world economic results.
The Second Committee has become a factory for “zombie resolutions” that reflect outdated economic paradigms and risk infringing upon national sovereignty. Business as usual is a luxury this institution can no longer afford. Moving forward, this Committee must enforce efficiency across four non-negotiable pillars:
First, Radical Consolidation. The Committee must end duplicative mandates that belong elsewhere, leave technical work to specialized agencies in Rome, Geneva, and Nairobi, and merge fragmented resolutions into lean omnibus frameworks.
Second, End the Annual Machine. The Committee must shift stable items to multi-year reviews. This is needed to break the cycle of the perpetual drafting machine.
Third, Text Streamlining. The Committee must enforce hard page limits, strip repetitive preambular clauses, and move historical references older than five years into footnotes. We are not historians – we are here to deliver measurable results.
Fourth, Stop Mandate Creep. Each resolution must fall into this committee’s core mandate on economic and financial issues. But this mandate has its limits. The UN must stop playing shadow regulator and respect the independent governance of the World Trade Organization, international financial institutions like the World Bank and IMF, and sovereign national regulatory bodies
These four pillars are not mere suggestions; they will define our engagement in this session.
During this session, the United States will engage selectively on resolutions, and we encourage others to do the same. Member States should not feel pressured to engage or rubber stamp every resolution raised this session.
We will continue to oppose resolutions that promote social engineering, push divisive political or social issues, or infringe on sovereignty.
We will oppose resolutions that position this committee as an administrative delivery mechanism for central planning or top-down global development targets.
Decades of government-to-government aid have often produced dependency, inefficiency, and corruption rather than lasting prosperity.
As Secretary Rubio stated, we must prioritize “trade over aid, opportunity over dependency, and investment over assistance.”
Real human flourishing comes from sovereign nations mobilizing their own resources, establishing the rule of law, protecting intellectual and physical property rights, and creating pro-growth, free-market environments. It is private business that builds successful economies, not UN decrees.
Expanding trade opportunities is a proven pathway to prosperity. By fostering fair and reciprocal trade relationships, we empower nations to attract investment, create jobs, and unlock private-sector-led growth.
In short, the Second Committee must pivot from managing dependency to fostering dynamism. The United States stands ready to work with delegations that seek to unlock private capital, champion innovation, and empower entrepreneurs.
Madam Chair, colleagues, we look forward to working with you in the weeks ahead. Thank you.
